## Category C: Task & Pipeline Intelligence

**9. Task-graph visualization needs a "cost overlay" — $/node on the DAG.**
Lines 960-1000 build a task graph from TASK_VERSIONS. The topology is shown, but there's no cost dimension on it. Overlay $/run per node (from MART_TASK_NODE_DAILY) so the reader sees which parts of the pipeline are expensive vs cheap.

*Reasoning:* A DAG without cost is a topology diagram. A DAG with cost is a budget allocation map. The latter tells you where to optimize.

**10. Add "pipeline SLA prediction" — will this table make its deadline?**
Line 589: Pipeline SLA shows current status (met/missed). But by the time it's missed, it's too late. Predict: "At current load rate, table X will miss its 8am SLA by ~20 minutes." Use the running average of load duration.

*Reasoning:* Reactive SLA monitoring ("it's already late") is less valuable than predictive ("it's going to be late"). A 30-minute warning gives the DBA time to act (bump warehouse size, skip optional transforms).

**11. The DQ row-volume monitor (line 525) should integrate with the Pipeline SLA view.**
Row-volume anomalies and pipeline freshness SLAs are related: if a table's row volume dropped 80%, it's probably because the upstream pipeline failed. Currently these are separate panels. Cross-link them: when a volume anomaly fires, check if the same table has a failing SLA.

*Reasoning:* Root-cause analysis requires correlating signals. A volume drop + an SLA miss on the same table is almost certainly the same incident. Showing them together saves investigation time.

