## Category E: Change Impact & Regression

**15. Change Impact should show a "blast radius preview" BEFORE a change is deployed.**
The current Change Impact section (line 1738) is reactive: it measures regressions AFTER a change happened. Add a "what-if" mode: "If I ALTER this table's clustering key, which queries will be affected?" using QUERY_HISTORY + ACCESS_HISTORY joins.

*Reasoning:* The best time to prevent a regression is before deployment. A pre-change blast radius estimate turns Change Impact from a post-mortem tool into a planning tool.

**16. Release Compare should auto-detect releases instead of requiring manual date input.**
Line 472: the user picks a release date manually. But you have DDL change detection (V010, V033) — you KNOW when schema changes happened. Auto-populate a dropdown of recent change events: "3 schema changes on Aug 12, 7 on Aug 9" and let the user pick from detected releases.

*Reasoning:* Manual date entry assumes the user knows when releases happened. Auto-detection assumes the system knows — which it does, from the change registry.

**17. Change Impact needs a "regression resolved?" status tracker.**
Currently it detects regressions. But there's no workflow for "regression detected → investigated → root cause found → fixed → verified back to baseline." Add a lifecycle status per regression finding so old, resolved regressions don't clutter the view.

*Reasoning:* Without lifecycle status, the change impact view accumulates findings forever. After a month, every regression is "open" even if fixed weeks ago. That trains users to ignore the section.

