## Category F: Usability & Performance

**18. The page is 1747 lines — split into sub-modules like Cost did.**
Cost refactored into `cost_parts/` (spend.py, contract.py, optimize.py, unit_costs.py). Operations at 1747 lines is harder to navigate, harder to test, and harder to maintain. Split into `ops_parts/queries.py`, `ops_parts/tasks.py`, `ops_parts/warehouses.py`, etc.

*Reasoning:* A 1747-line file with 8 section functions, each 100-300 lines, is a maintenance burden. Changes to the task section risk breaking the query section through merge conflicts.

**19. The incident routing panel (line 417) should link to the Control Room incident timeline.**
When a task failure is routed to an incident, the DBA should be able to click through to the incident on the Control Room timeline to see the full ±30min context. Currently the routing panel is self-contained — it doesn't connect to the broader incident model.

*Reasoning:* The incident is the same object whether viewed from Operations (task failure perspective) or Control Room (timeline perspective). Cross-linking them creates a unified investigation flow.

**20. Add "Operations score" that complements the platform score.**
The platform score (Overview) is a composite. Operations doesn't have its own domain score. Create one: "Operations health: 82/100" with named deductions (query fail rate above threshold: -5, task failures: -8, queue time: -4, stale pipelines: -3). Show it at the top of the page.

*Reasoning:* A domain-specific score lets the Ops team track THEIR health over time, independent of cost or security. It also creates accountability: "our operations score dropped from 88 to 74 this week — what happened?"

